1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
Distinguished guests, and all who are willing to listen across borders,

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:08,880
thank you for gathering around a difficult question. What does it

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:13,320
mean to speak responsibly of peace while war continues? We do

4
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:17,760
not meet to pretend that a speech can stop missiles, restore

5
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:22,200
a destroyed home, or bring back a child. Words cannot do

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,639
those things. But words can clarify what we owe one another,

7
00:00:26,639 --> 00:00:31,079
and clarity can guide action. Tonight I will make a simple

8
00:00:31,079 --> 00:00:35,519
argument. Peace is not a miracle we wait for. It is

9
00:00:35,519 --> 00:00:39,959
a practice we must design, examine, and sustain. It begins with

10
00:00:39,959 --> 00:00:44,399
human dignity, continues through justice, and becomes credible only when promises

11
00:00:44,399 --> 00:00:45,610
can be verified.

12
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:50,890
The war against Ukraine gives these ideas an immediate moral weight.

13
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:55,771
Russia's full-scale invasion in 2022 violated the basic principle that a

14
00:00:55,771 --> 00:01:00,651
country must not take another country's territory by force. That principle

15
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:05,531
protects every nation, large or small. Yet the suffering of civilians

16
00:01:05,531 --> 00:01:10,412
is never just an illustration of international law. It is the

17
00:01:10,412 --> 00:01:15,292
destroyed routines of ordinary people: a kitchen without a family, a

18
00:01:15,292 --> 00:01:20,172
classroom without pupils, a street where a child learns to fear

19
00:01:20,172 --> 00:01:25,053
the sky. To insist on Ukraine's sovereignty is not to deny

20
00:01:25,053 --> 00:01:29,933
the humanity of Russian civilians. It is to insist that security

21
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:34,813
cannot be built by making another people surrender their right to

22
00:01:34,813 --> 00:01:36,588
choose their own future.

23
00:01:36,988 --> 00:01:41,743
We should reject three convenient illusions. The first is that peace

24
00:01:41,743 --> 00:01:46,498
means simply the absence of gunfire. A pause that conceals preparations

25
00:01:46,498 --> 00:01:51,254
for renewed violence may be valuable for saving lives, but it

26
00:01:51,254 --> 00:01:56,009
is not yet a durable settlement. The second illusion is that

27
00:01:56,009 --> 00:02:00,764
peace requires us to forget questions of responsibility. Forgetting may silence

28
00:02:00,764 --> 00:02:05,519
a discussion; it does not restore trust. The third illusion is

29
00:02:05,519 --> 00:02:10,275
that disagreement itself is the enemy. Disagreement is often the beginning

30
00:02:10,275 --> 00:02:15,030
of serious public reasoning. Our challenge is not to make everyone

31
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:19,785
speak with one voice. It is to build conditions under which

32
00:02:19,785 --> 00:02:24,540
people can disagree without threatening each other, and can assess the

33
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:27,134
same evidence without losing their dignity.

34
00:02:27,534 --> 00:02:32,562
Consider a principle familiar in moral philosophy: every person has worth

35
00:02:32,562 --> 00:02:37,590
that cannot be reduced to a government's strategic advantage. Call it

36
00:02:37,590 --> 00:02:42,619
dignity, equal moral standing, or the refusal to use people merely

37
00:02:42,619 --> 00:02:47,647
as instruments. Whatever vocabulary we choose, the implication is concrete. Civilians

38
00:02:47,647 --> 00:02:52,675
are not bargaining chips. Prisoners are not objects of public spectacle.

39
00:02:52,675 --> 00:02:57,703
The displaced are not statistics that can be moved across a

40
00:02:57,703 --> 00:03:02,732
map without consent. This principle does not resolve every military and

41
00:03:02,732 --> 00:03:07,760
diplomatic problem. It does something more basic. It sets limits on

42
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:12,788
what we may justify even when the situation is frightening. When

43
00:03:12,788 --> 00:03:17,816
decisions involve risk, the people who bear that risk deserve visibility,

44
00:03:17,816 --> 00:03:21,016
protection, and the opportunity to be heard.

45
00:03:21,416 --> 00:03:26,435
Some say we must choose between justice and peace, as if

46
00:03:26,435 --> 00:03:31,454
the two were enemies. The tension is real. Negotiators may face

47
00:03:31,454 --> 00:03:36,474
urgent choices between preventing further deaths and pursuing full accountability. But

48
00:03:36,474 --> 00:03:41,493
this does not justify treating justice as an ornament to be

49
00:03:41,493 --> 00:03:46,512
added later, if convenient. A sustainable arrangement needs credible protection, transparent

50
00:03:46,512 --> 00:03:51,531
evidence, and procedures through which wrongs may be addressed. It must

51
00:03:51,531 --> 00:03:56,551
also distinguish the responsibility of leaders and armed actors from the

52
00:03:56,551 --> 00:04:01,570
dignity of ordinary people. We cannot promise that every injustice will

53
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:06,589
be remedied immediately. We can promise not to build a political

54
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:11,608
future upon the denial that injustice occurred. That commitment matters for

55
00:04:11,608 --> 00:04:14,346
peace in Ukraine and everywhere else.

56
00:04:14,746 --> 00:04:19,898
My first proposal is a framework for verifiable de-escalation. Any ceasefire

57
00:04:19,898 --> 00:04:25,051
worth trusting should identify who monitors it, what areas and activities

58
00:04:25,051 --> 00:04:30,203
it covers, how alleged violations are reported, and how civilians retain

59
00:04:30,203 --> 00:04:35,356
access to essential aid. Independent observers must be able to collect

60
00:04:35,356 --> 00:04:40,508
evidence under conditions that protect their safety and impartiality. All parties

61
00:04:40,508 --> 00:04:45,660
should know the channels for urgent communication and the procedures for

62
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:50,813
investigating incidents. No monitoring system can eliminate mistrust by itself. But

63
00:04:50,813 --> 00:04:55,965
it can replace some rumor with information and some improvisation with

64
00:04:55,965 --> 00:05:01,118
an agreed procedure. A ceasefire should never be advertised as a

65
00:05:01,118 --> 00:05:06,270
finished peace. It is a potentially life-saving step whose conditions must

66
00:05:06,270 --> 00:05:08,612
be clear and publicly scrutinized.

67
00:05:09,012 --> 00:05:14,653
My second proposal concerns protection that can begin even before a

68
00:05:14,653 --> 00:05:20,294
comprehensive political agreement. Hospitals, medical workers, schools, emergency shelters, and humanitarian

69
00:05:20,294 --> 00:05:25,935
routes require special attention. Mechanisms for locating missing persons and supporting

70
00:05:25,935 --> 00:05:31,576
family reunification deserve resources, documentation, and independent oversight. Prisoner exchanges and

71
00:05:31,576 --> 00:05:37,217
the safe return of unlawfully transferred or displaced people should be

72
00:05:37,217 --> 00:05:42,858
guided by international humanitarian and human rights law. Those who seek

73
00:05:42,858 --> 00:05:48,499
refuge must not be treated as props in a contest of

74
00:05:48,499 --> 00:05:54,140
public relations. Help should follow need, not loyalty. When families receive

75
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:59,781
reliable information about a missing relative, or when a patient can

76
00:05:59,781 --> 00:06:05,422
safely reach a hospital, political language begins to acquire practical meaning.

77
00:06:05,822 --> 00:06:11,041
My third proposal is a reconstruction process that people can trust.

78
00:06:11,041 --> 00:06:16,260
Rebuilding infrastructure means far more than replacing concrete. It means restoring

79
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:21,478
schools, utilities, clinics, cultural spaces, and the relationships that make a

80
00:06:21,478 --> 00:06:26,697
community possible. Funding decisions should be open to audit. Contracts should

81
00:06:26,697 --> 00:06:31,916
be traceable. Communities directly affected by destruction should have meaningful ways

82
00:06:31,916 --> 00:06:37,135
to participate in planning, including people with disabilities, older residents, and

83
00:06:37,135 --> 00:06:42,354
those who have been displaced. Evidence relevant to possible war crimes

84
00:06:42,354 --> 00:06:47,572
must be preserved, not discarded in the rush to rebuild. Reconstruction

85
00:06:47,572 --> 00:06:52,791
and accountability can reinforce each other. A repaired bridge is an

86
00:06:52,791 --> 00:06:58,010
achievement, but so is a public institution that can explain honestly

87
00:06:58,010 --> 00:07:02,280
how the bridge was financed and whom it serves.

88
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:08,010
My fourth proposal addresses the problem every peace negotiator must face:

89
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:13,341
what happens after the agreement? Words such as guarantee, deterrence, and

90
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:18,671
neutrality may sound decisive while hiding unanswered questions. Who acts if

91
00:07:18,671 --> 00:07:24,002
a promise is broken? What facts trigger a response? Which institutions

92
00:07:24,002 --> 00:07:29,332
have authority? What safeguards prevent an incident from becoming a wider

93
00:07:29,332 --> 00:07:34,663
catastrophe? These questions cannot be solved through eloquence alone. They require

94
00:07:34,663 --> 00:07:39,993
negotiation, legal analysis, democratic accountability, and recognition of the people whose

95
00:07:39,993 --> 00:07:45,324
lives are most directly affected. Security is not merely the power

96
00:07:45,324 --> 00:07:50,654
to threaten an opponent. At its best, security gives communities reliable

97
00:07:50,654 --> 00:07:55,985
reasons to believe that tomorrow will not bring the same terror

98
00:07:55,985 --> 00:07:56,954
as yesterday.

99
00:07:57,354 --> 00:08:02,677
My fifth proposal is to give public reasoning a real place

100
00:08:02,677 --> 00:08:08,001
in the peace process. Civilians should not be expected to sign

101
00:08:08,001 --> 00:08:13,324
an imaginary agreement written for them by distant spectators. Researchers, educators,

102
00:08:13,324 --> 00:08:18,648
legal experts, local communities, and those directly affected by violence can

103
00:08:18,648 --> 00:08:23,971
contribute evidence and criticism. Their participation should not be a decorative

104
00:08:23,971 --> 00:08:29,295
consultation after decisions have already been made. Publish proposals in accessible

105
00:08:29,295 --> 00:08:34,618
language. Disclose uncertainties. Explain the trade-offs. Allow objections to be recorded

106
00:08:34,618 --> 00:08:39,942
and addressed. This is not a call for endless discussion while

107
00:08:39,942 --> 00:08:45,265
people suffer. It is a call to make urgent decisions more

108
00:08:45,265 --> 00:08:50,588
responsible by exposing them to informed scrutiny. Genuine consensus can emerge

109
00:08:50,588 --> 00:08:54,460
without silencing anyone who asks a hard question.

110
00:08:54,860 --> 00:09:00,009
There is another duty, especially when images and voices can be

111
00:09:00,009 --> 00:09:05,158
synthesized with extraordinary realism. A beautiful performance may persuade us before

112
00:09:05,158 --> 00:09:10,307
we ask whether its claims are true. We must resist confusing

113
00:09:10,307 --> 00:09:15,456
emotional conviction with verified fact. When a speaker refers to events,

114
00:09:15,456 --> 00:09:20,605
sources should be named. When a proposal is speculative, it should

115
00:09:20,605 --> 00:09:25,754
be introduced as a proposal, not as a completed diplomatic agreement.

116
00:09:25,754 --> 00:09:30,903
When evidence is uncertain, uncertainty should be stated openly. This address

117
00:09:30,903 --> 00:09:36,051
itself is an original cinematic production, not a recording of an

118
00:09:36,051 --> 00:09:41,200
official meeting. That distinction matters. The value of a moral argument

119
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:46,349
comes from the reasons and evidence available for examining it, not

120
00:09:46,349 --> 00:09:50,094
from how convincingly a camera can portray authority.

121
00:09:50,494 --> 00:09:55,194
We often speak of love as a private feeling, belonging to

122
00:09:55,194 --> 00:09:59,893
families, friendships, and moments of intimacy. Yet there is also a

123
00:09:59,893 --> 00:10:04,593
public meaning of care. To care for strangers is to recognize

124
00:10:04,593 --> 00:10:09,293
their vulnerability even when they are far away and even when

125
00:10:09,293 --> 00:10:13,992
they do not share our language or beliefs. But care without

126
00:10:13,992 --> 00:10:18,692
judgment can become sentimentality. We need reason to ask whether an

127
00:10:18,692 --> 00:10:23,392
intervention helps, whether a policy creates new dangers, and whether institutions

128
00:10:23,392 --> 00:10:28,091
can correct mistakes. Love may be the motive that makes us

129
00:10:28,091 --> 00:10:32,791
refuse indifference. Law, evidence, and accountable institutions are among the means

130
00:10:32,791 --> 00:10:37,491
by which that refusal becomes durable protection. Neither compassion nor critical

131
00:10:37,491 --> 00:10:41,336
reason should be asked to do the other's work.

132
00:10:41,736 --> 00:10:46,649
A serious peace process requires listening to people whose perspectives disturb

133
00:10:46,649 --> 00:10:51,561
us. Listening is not the same as conceding that all claims

134
00:10:51,561 --> 00:10:56,474
are equally justified. The invasion of another sovereign country cannot be

135
00:10:56,474 --> 00:11:01,387
excused merely because a leader describes fear or grievance. At the

136
00:11:01,387 --> 00:11:06,299
same time, understanding how fear, historical memory, and misinformation shape political

137
00:11:06,299 --> 00:11:11,212
choices may help prevent future violence. The distinction is essential. We

138
00:11:11,212 --> 00:11:16,125
can defend a clear legal and moral boundary while investigating the

139
00:11:16,125 --> 00:11:21,037
social conditions that make conflict endure. The purpose of dialogue is

140
00:11:21,037 --> 00:11:25,950
not to erase responsibility. It is to learn what must change

141
00:11:25,950 --> 00:11:30,863
if responsible agreements are to survive. We should be firm about

142
00:11:30,863 --> 00:11:34,882
rights and humble about the limits of our knowledge.

143
00:11:35,282 --> 00:11:40,406
What can an ordinary citizen do? Most of us cannot sit

144
00:11:40,406 --> 00:11:45,530
at a negotiating table. We cannot order an army to withdraw

145
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:50,654
or guarantee a treaty. Still, we can refuse to circulate unverified

146
00:11:50,654 --> 00:11:55,778
stories. We can support credible humanitarian relief, welcome people displaced by

147
00:11:55,778 --> 00:12:00,902
violence, ask public officials to explain their decisions, and examine claims

148
00:12:00,902 --> 00:12:06,025
from our own preferred side with the same seriousness we demand

149
00:12:06,025 --> 00:12:11,149
of others. Educators can teach students to distinguish an argument from

150
00:12:11,149 --> 00:12:16,273
a slogan. Journalists can document uncertainty instead of rewarding spectacle. Communities

151
00:12:16,273 --> 00:12:21,397
can keep space for difficult conversations. These actions are small compared

152
00:12:21,397 --> 00:12:26,521
with the scale of war. Small does not mean meaningless, especially

153
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:29,316
when responsible habits spread across institutions.

154
00:12:29,716 --> 00:12:34,938
Imagine that every proposal made tonight were accompanied by a public

155
00:12:34,938 --> 00:12:40,160
record of its purpose, its assumptions, its risks, and its evidence.

156
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:45,381
For civilian protection, we might monitor access to medical care and

157
00:12:45,381 --> 00:12:50,603
the number of verified attacks on protected facilities, while protecting sensitive

158
00:12:50,603 --> 00:12:55,825
personal information. For humanitarian return, we could track consent, safety, and

159
00:12:55,825 --> 00:13:01,047
successful reunification rather than count people moved across borders. For rebuilding,

160
00:13:01,047 --> 00:13:06,268
we could publish independent audits and community feedback. For negotiation, we

161
00:13:06,268 --> 00:13:11,490
could report what has been agreed and what remains disputed. No

162
00:13:11,490 --> 00:13:16,712
metric can capture the full reality of human suffering. But without

163
00:13:16,712 --> 00:13:21,934
careful measures, governments and citizens may confuse an inspiring announcement with

164
00:13:21,934 --> 00:13:24,782
a real improvement in people's lives.

165
00:13:25,182 --> 00:13:29,901
Hope is sometimes criticized as wishful thinking, a refusal to admit

166
00:13:29,901 --> 00:13:34,620
how stubbornly institutions fail. But there is another kind of hope.

167
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:39,338
It begins by naming reality without decoration. It recognizes that human

168
00:13:39,338 --> 00:13:44,057
beings make mistakes, that interests conflict, and that a good proposal

169
00:13:44,057 --> 00:13:48,776
may be rejected. Then it asks what responsible action remains possible.

170
00:13:48,776 --> 00:13:53,495
Such hope is not a forecast that peace will arrive soon.

171
00:13:53,495 --> 00:13:58,214
It is a commitment to keep seeking better reasons and better

172
00:13:58,214 --> 00:14:02,932
safeguards. In philosophy, we often learn that a question becomes fruitful

173
00:14:02,932 --> 00:14:07,651
when its assumptions are examined. The same is true in public

174
00:14:07,651 --> 00:14:12,370
life. Asking how peace might be tested can be more helpful

175
00:14:12,370 --> 00:14:14,944
than announcing that peace is inevitable.

176
00:14:15,344 --> 00:14:20,214
If you disagree with the proposals I have offered, I invite

177
00:14:20,214 --> 00:14:25,083
you to say why. Perhaps a monitoring plan overlooks a security

178
00:14:25,083 --> 00:14:29,953
risk. Perhaps a reconstruction proposal lacks a way to include displaced

179
00:14:29,953 --> 00:14:34,822
communities. Perhaps a phrase that sounds fair to one listener hides

180
00:14:34,822 --> 00:14:39,692
a painful assumption for another. These objections should not be treated

181
00:14:39,692 --> 00:14:44,561
as disloyalty. They are opportunities to make the argument more exact.

182
00:14:44,561 --> 00:14:49,431
We need neither unanimity nor theatrical outrage. We need honest questions,

183
00:14:49,431 --> 00:14:54,300
accessible evidence, and institutions willing to change course. A peaceful world

184
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:59,170
cannot be constructed by forbidding people to disagree. It must make

185
00:14:59,170 --> 00:15:04,039
room for disagreement without allowing disagreement to become a license for

186
00:15:04,039 --> 00:15:04,482
harm.

187
00:15:04,882 --> 00:15:09,403
Let me close with a promise that does not ask you

188
00:15:09,403 --> 00:15:13,923
to believe in miracles. We will not pretend that a film

189
00:15:13,923 --> 00:15:18,444
can replace diplomacy, or that a moving voice can bring a

190
00:15:18,444 --> 00:15:22,964
war to an end. We will instead hold ourselves to a

191
00:15:22,964 --> 00:15:27,485
more demanding standard. Defend sovereignty. Protect civilians. Seek verifiable agreements. Support

192
00:15:27,485 --> 00:15:32,005
reconstruction that answers to the people it serves. Keep evidence and

193
00:15:32,005 --> 00:15:36,526
accountability in view. Welcome criticism. Let compassion become an obligation, and

194
00:15:36,526 --> 00:15:41,046
let reason help that obligation survive. We can begin with words,

195
00:15:41,046 --> 00:15:45,567
but we must not end with them. Peace is not a

196
00:15:45,567 --> 00:15:50,087
miracle we wait for. It is a responsibility we learn to

197
00:15:50,087 --> 00:15:52,964
share, one honest commitment at a time.
